    <h2 class="section-title">Highlights</h2>
    <h3 class="sub-title">What are the main findings?</h3>
    <ul class="findings">
      <li>Differencing-based transformations (first, seasonal, fractional) act as high-pass filters that remove predictable low-frequency signal, worsening forecast accuracy in the majority of cases&mdash;even for models traditionally assumed to require stationary input.</li>
      <li>Variance stabilization (log, Box-Cox) is the primary exception, improving forecasts on heteroscedastic data by addressing a modeling assumption violation without attenuating predictive structure.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="sub-title">What are the implications of the main findings?</h3>
    <ul class="findings">
      <li>Transformation selection should be guided by empirical out-of-sample evaluation rather than theoretical stationarity assumptions.</li>
      <li>Practitioners should distinguish between signal-preserving transforms (variance stabilization) and signal-suppressing transforms (differencing), complementing theoretical guidance with empirical out-of-sample validation.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="section-title">Abstract</h2>
    <p class="abstract-text">
      Stationarity transformations are standard preprocessing in time series forecasting, yet their actual impact on accuracy across different non-stationarity types and model families has received little controlled evaluation. We construct synthetic datasets with known properties&mdash;trend, seasonality, heteroscedasticity, and combinations&mdash;and apply fourteen transformation configurations across seven models and three forecast horizons (3,528 experiments). Stationarity is quantified via consensus ratios from ten statistical tests, and each transform&ndash;dataset pair is classified as matched or mismatched based on whether the transform targets the dataset's known non-stationarity. For matched pairs, transforms improve forecasts only 18% of the time. The primary exception is variance stabilization: log and Box-Cox on heteroscedastic data improve accuracy in 60&ndash;65% of cases. Differencing a linear-trend series&mdash;a textbook use case&mdash;worsens forecasts in all cases tested. Mediation analysis confirms that while transforms achieve trend stationarity, this does not translate into lower forecast error; the mechanism is signal attenuation. Real-world validation on TSA airport passenger data corroborates these findings. Our results suggest transformation selection should be guided by empirical out-of-sample evaluation rather than theoretical stationarity assumptions.
    </p>

    <p class="keywords"><strong>Keywords:</strong> stationarity transformations; forecast accuracy; time series preprocessing; controlled experiment; mediation analysis; fractional differencing; synthetic data</p>

